Hi Chris,
I was first tempted to use each bird's trip as a unit but do now consider the individual level because of the subsequent overlap() analysis. I have an average DOF[area] for my trips of 4.3, an average of 29.2 locations per trip, and an average number of trips per bird of 4.0. I am not however 100% sure which "unit" the DOF estimates refer too? In Fleming and Calabrese (2017), the "Effective sample sizes" paragraph mentions that "the effective sample size of the bandwidth ^nH is also the number of sampled locations n, as is the effective sample size of the area ^nA". As I understood, however, this was for the IID data. Is the DOF[area] specifically refer to the number of sampled locations in non-IID data?
Curiosity: Since the DOF[area] is representative of the effective sample size and thus data representativity, would that be possible to use the DOF[area] as a weighting component in the AKDE calculation?
I will take a closer look at the potential discrepancies between the model selected from the information criteria and "LOOCV". So far, the differences were not that striking.
Regarding the CIs for the ctmm(sigma=30 %#% "km^2",tau=c(5%#%"hours",5%#%"hours"),omega=2*pi/(1%#%"days"), isotropic = T, circle = T) model: In rare cases, although the models converged, the estimates appeared overinflated e.g. est (low:high) for speed (kilometers/day) 5 219 (3167:7272) and T[period] (years) Inf (Inf:Inf). However, the model fit is obviously poor in such cases (see figure below). The Ouf models perform better in those cases.
Regarding the "bandwidth effect a greater lag": I was wondering about the correction level on the points located at the tail of the distributions (e.g. figures from the previous message) which are sometimes under the asymptotic model CIs. These points at the tail are also often related to the positions close to the colony. Would that impose an "overcorrection" on those lower autocorrelated points?
Thank you once more for your time, very appreciated. Best, /Phil

